  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(9)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{9}+C \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}-2.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$",
